feat(wear): the wrist shows WHICH card is asking, and can answer it

The mirror already reached /wear/chat in the previous commit; the watch just
had nowhere to put it. Line carried only (mine, text, ts, date), so a card's
question rendered under the name "Henry" - a lie the owner acts on, because he
reads a worker waiting on a decision as Henry giving advice.

* the label ("Frage · Kartenname") replaces the sender name on a mirrored
  event. Composed on the WATCH from the parts the daemon ships (kind +
  cardName), never pre-rendered server-side: the phone draws the same parts as
  a transcript sender line, and a server that had guessed one layout would be
  wrong on the other surface.
* option buttons sit under the question that offered them, not pooled at the
  bottom with Henry's own follow-ups. Two cards can wait at once, and a pooled
  list gives no way to see which card a button belongs to.
* tapping one calls /wear/talk with `reply_to_card`, so it settles THAT card
  through sessions.reply_door. Without it the tap would send a bare "A" into
  Henry's advisory session - which cannot settle another agent's question,
  reads to Henry as a non-sequitur, and leaves the card waiting.
* labels ride VERBATIM into the answer: ask.validate_answers matches by
  equality, so a label trimmed for the small screen would silently stop being
  a preset pick and arrive as the owner's own free text.
* a failed answer puts the buttons BACK and says so. An answer that never
  landed must not look like one that did, or the owner walks away from a card
  still waiting on him.
* DeviceStore caches `label` (a restored card line would otherwise render as
  "Henry" for the moment before the server transcript arrives) but NOT the
  card id or its options - offering a tap on a question whose current state
  has not been re-read is how a stale answer happens.

NOT VERIFIED, and this is the same limit the wear backlog card already
records: no Android SDK, Gradle or Kotlin compiler is reachable from this
worktree, so none of this Kotlin has been compiled - the `:wear` module never
has been. Balanced brackets were checked and are deliberately NOT counted as
verification. The daemon half it talks to IS verified
(ops/tests/test_chat_mirror.py: the /wear/chat payload carries the label parts
and verbatim options, and a wrist reply routes to answer/steer).

run_gate PASS; chat_mirror, lane_visibility, presence_notify, chat_date_stamp,
